messages:
maria: Repro note — Expo managed sample intermittently fails after fast refresh with auth/retry errors. Error: 'Cannot read property 'headers' of undefined' from our retry layer. Cold start ok, hot reload breaks. Anyone have quick theories?

sam: sounds like two different fetch instances or a cloned response losing custom props. If the SDK bundle includes its own fetch shim (CJS) while the app uses global fetch, you can get inconsistent identity. Does package.json expose a react-native entry?

maria: not yet. package.json has main -> dist/index.cjs.js and module -> dist/index.mjs. No react-native export. The sample imports the SDK normally.

jess: Metro sometimes resolves the CJS main for RN which can bundle a minimal fetch polyfill. That will produce two fetch worlds. Fast refresh can re-evaluate modules out of order, leaving the auth interceptor unattached when the SDK makes a request.

sam: fix options: 1) add a synchronous RN entry that sets up `global.fetch` and the auth interceptor, then re-export SDK; add `exports["react-native"]` to package.json pointing at it; 2) publish ESM-first builds so Metro dedupes more reliably.

maria: ok — thinking of a tiny `dist/rn/setup-fetch.js`:

```js
// dist/rn/setup-fetch.js
if (typeof globalThis.fetch !== 'function') {
  globalThis.fetch = require('cross-fetch');
}
// attach auth wrapper synchronously
const orig = globalThis.fetch;
globalThis.fetch = function (input, init) {
  init = init || {};
  init.headers = Object.assign({}, init.headers, { Authorization: process.env.TEST_TOKEN || '' });
  return orig(input, init);
};
module.exports = require('../index.cjs');
```

That way any consumer import runs the setup first.

oliver: good. Also add a guard for fast refresh: `if (!globalThis.__redwood_fetch_setup) { /* setup */ globalThis.__redwood_fetch_setup = true }` so repeated evals don't duplicate wrappers.

jess: typings: TS in RN sample complains about `import Redwood from '@redwood/inference'` unless esModuleInterop. Our published `.d.ts` uses `export =` shape which forces `import * as` style. We should publish `index.d.ts` with `export default` for ESM consumers and keep legacy cjs types for require.

sam: use conditional exports for types too: "types": "./dist/index.d.ts" plus have the d.ts describe default + named exports. Example top-level exports in package.json:

```
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/index.cjs.js",
    "react-native": "./dist/rn/setup-fetch.js"
  }
}
```

That usually helps TS + Metro pick the RN path.

maria: one more runtime gotcha — some RN polyfills implement `Response.prototype.clone` poorly and strip internal fields we rely on in retry wrapper. If you rely on `response.clone()` make sure the same fetch implementation is used everywhere.

jess: dev tip: in the sample README add a short onboarding note: "If using Expo managed, ensure SDK >= X and Metro >= Y so conditional exports are honored, or import '@redwood/inference/rn' directly to guarantee RN entry."

oliver: I'll draft a tiny PR that adds `dist/rn/setup-fetch.js`, updates package.json exports, and replaces the sample's SDK import with the RN path for now. We'll follow up by publishing ESM-first build and revisiting types.

maria: thanks — landing that should reduce onboarding friction. opened ticket #934 for the RN onboarding flake and linked this thread.

devex-bot: CI notice: expo-managed iOS fast-refresh tests flaked; ticket #934 created.

maria: ping @sam once PR is ready for quick review. :pray:
